/CaselIT","html_url":"https://github.com/CaselIT","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2021-10-02T12:59:44Z","updated_at":"2021-10-02T13:00:19Z","body":"Also looking at the example, it seems that you are using the loaded object after the session context has exited. It's probably not the issue, but you should manipulate the objects obtained from the session in it's condext:\r\n\r\n```py\r\ndef main():\r\n    engine = sa.create_engine(\"sqlite+pysqlite:///:memory:\", future=True)\r\n    set_up_example_data(engine)\r\n\r\n    with orm.Session(engine) as session:\r\n        child = session.query(Child).options(\r\n            orm.joinedload(Child.parent),\r\n            orm.raiseload(\"*\"),\r\n        ).first()\r\n\r\n        # This raises an AttributeError:\r\n        dumps(child)\r\n```","author_association":"MEMBER","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/sqlalchemy/sqlalchemy/issues/comments/932748370/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"CaselIT","id":16175304,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MTc1MzA0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16175304?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/CaselIT","html_url":"https://github.com/CaselIT","followers